{{!-- One step of a Seasons Change move, with whatever control the sheet can offer for it.

      The kinds come from SeasonProcedure, which reads them off the move. Handlebars has no
      polymorphism, so the branch is here — but it is the ONLY one: everything a step differs by
      (its label, its dice, how many it offers) is answered by the step itself, and this partial
      only asks which control to draw.

      Context is a SeasonStepSnapshot — the step, plus everything this steading brings to it: the dice
      it actually rolls, what it actually pays, what it has already done, and the choice it calls for.
      It is passed WITHOUT hash params on purpose: a partial invoked with hash args gets a flattened
      copy of its context, and a step answers everything about itself through prototype getters —
      every one of which is lost in that copy, silently, leaving a numbered row with no label and no
      control. --}}
<li class="steading-turn-step">
	<span class="steading-turn-num" aria-hidden="true">{{number}}</span>
	<div class="steading-turn-body-step">

		{{!-- The move's own words for this step. `labelKey` is the fallback, for a homebrew Seasons
		      Change whose steps carry no authored text: "Roll it" reads poorly next to "when winter
		      grips the land", but it beats a numbered row with no line at all. --}}
		{{#if text}}
			<div class="steading-turn-text stonetop-rich">{{rich text}}</div>
		{{else}}
			<h4 class="steading-turn-label">
				{{localize labelKey die=die count=count stat=(localize statLabelKey)}}
			</h4>

			{{#if noteKey}}
				<p class="steading-turn-note">{{localize noteKey die=die}}</p>
			{{/if}}
		{{/if}}

		{{!-- Every control below carries a short verb where the step has the move's own words, and the
		      descriptive label only where it does not. The line has just said whose roll it is, at what,
		      and why; a button repeating "Roll +Fortunes" under "Then, roll +Fortunes." is the sentence
		      twice.

		      The move's own roll and a step's own dice are two different controls, and the difference is
		      what they write: the first posts a card, the second moves Surplus. Neither turns the
		      wheel — that is the advance control below the box, and it rolls nothing. --}}

		{{#if isTieredRoll}}
			{{!-- The move's own roll, as a NORMAL move roll: the 2d6+Fortunes with its 10+/7-9/6- tiers,
			      posted to chat, leaving the wheel exactly where it stands.

			      This is where Seasons Change is rolled. The turn control below only advances the wheel,
			      so the roll is the table's to make when they reach it — as many times as the season
			      asks for. Goes through the same `.rollable[data-roll]` path as every other move on the
			      sheet, addressed by slug — so the tiers, the debilities and the roll mode are the ones
			      every move gets. --}}
			<button type="button" class="rollable move-rollable steading-turn-roll is-plain"
			        data-view-state data-roll="{{step.stat}}"
			        data-move-slug="{{@root.stonetop.seasons.currentMove.slug}}">
				<i class="fas fa-dice-d6" aria-hidden="true"></i>
				<span>{{#if text}}{{localize "stonetop.steading.seasons.steps.rollShort"}}{{else}}{{localize labelKey}}{{/if}}</span>
			</button>

			{{!-- What the move says happens on each result, as three rows rather than one sentence
			      holding all three. The words are the move's OWN result tiers — the same ones its chat
			      card prints — so nothing here is authored twice.

			      A row rolls where its tier costs the steading a roll: winter's 7-9 and 6- each consume
			      another 1d4+Population, which was prose in the middle of a clause, with no control, no
			      record and no way to undo it, beside a first consumption that had all three. --}}
			{{#if hasTiers}}
				{{!-- Drawn by the partial the move's own item sheet draws its results with: one move,
				      one set of three results, one way of reading them. The row the DICE landed on is
				      lit — the season keeps what its move came up, so what the table is living with is
				      the row that says so rather than a tier they have to remember.

				      What the row COSTS — the clauses waiting on this result, the roll the tier
				      consumes — is the tier's own body partial, handed the tier itself so its getters
				      survive the trip (see stonetop.move-result-row). --}}
				<div class="steading-turn-tiers stonetop-result-rows">
					{{#each tiers}}
						{{> stonetop.move-result-row key=key label=label text=text rolled=isRolled
						    bodyPartial="stonetop.steading-tier-body" body=this}}
					{{/each}}
				</div>
			{{/if}}
		{{/if}}

		{{!-- What the steading has built that belongs to THIS step — the mill's +1 and Greater
		      Harvest's 1d4 at the autumn harvest, the town's Population+1 where the season generates.
		      Read here, and paid here: the amounts are inside the control below rather than in a panel
		      under the list with an Apply of its own, which had the table paying one event twice in
		      two places.

		      No control on any row: the step's own control is the act, and reverting it gives back
		      everything it wrote. Stated through the statement partial with nothing in its applicable
		      list, so a moment that hands the table a MOVE still shows it as the move it is. --}}
		{{#if hasContribution}}
			<div class="steading-turn-moment">
				{{#with contribution}}
					{{> "stonetop.steading-statement" statement=statement advisoryLines=lines
					    moveSlugs=statement.grantedMoveSlugs
					    showSource=true showDelta=false bulleted=true}}
				{{/with}}
			</div>
		{{/if}}

		{{#if movesSurplus}}
			{{!-- Any step that MOVES Surplus: winter's 1d4+Population, summer's 1d4-1, autumn's 1d4 at
			      the harvest, and the one the sheet adds for the steading's own gains in a season whose
			      move generates nothing. Winter's had no control at all — the panel's one button rolled
			      +Fortunes, which is winter's FOURTH step — and the other two had none either, which
			      only became obvious once the step said "roll 1d4" in the book's own words. --}}

			{{!-- What the steading's SIZE makes of the dice — 1d2+Population in a hamlet, 2d6+Population
			      in a town. The step's own line is the book's and says 1d4, so a control offering 2d6
			      with nothing between them would read as the sheet disagreeing with the move it draws.
			      Shown only where the size actually changes them. --}}
			{{#if hasSizeRoll}}
				{{#with sizeRoll}}
					<p class="steading-turn-size">
						{{localize labelKey size=(localize sizeLabelKey) die=die stat=(localize statLabelKey)}}
					</p>
				{{/with}}
			{{/if}}

			{{!-- What this steading has built that bends this step — each in the book's own sentence,
			      under the step it bends. "Consider Population to be 1 lower than it is." "Consumes 1
			      less Surplus than normal."

			      The sheet does NOT narrate what it made of them. A first attempt printed its own
			      summary — "Rolls 2d6 + Population, counted −1 — Additional Housing, Township" — which
			      stacked a second formula under the move's own, said neither which improvement did
			      which nor what was counted −1, and meant nothing to a reader. What the sheet made of
			      them is on the control below, which rolls the dice it names. --}}
			{{#if hasAdjustments}}
				{{> "stonetop.steading-effect-lines" lines=adjustments extraClass="steading-turn-results"}}
			{{/if}}

			{{> "stonetop.steading-turn-roll" }}
		{{/if}}

		{{#if isReset}}
			{{#if @root.editable}}
				{{!-- The value, always. The step's line is the book's — "reset Fortunes to +1" — but a
				      malcontent steading resets to +0, and this control is the only thing that knows
				      it. Shortened, not stripped. --}}
				<button type="button" class="steading-turn-reset" data-action="resetFortunes">
					{{#if text}}
						{{localize "stonetop.steading.seasons.steps.resetShort" value=@root.stonetop.fortunesReset}}
					{{else}}
						{{localize "stonetop.steading.seasons.resetFortunes" value=@root.stonetop.fortunesReset}}
					{{/if}}
				</button>
			{{/if}}
		{{/if}}

		{{#if isPick}}
			{{!-- The choice, made where the move says to make it. It used to be a note pointing at a
			      block further down the tab — "below, where it stays in force all season" — which named
			      the wrong place for the one thing the step is about.

			      It can only be here because the box is the CURRENT season's: spring, summer and autumn
			      all write the same `seasonal-gains` group and turning the wheel clears it, so a picker
			      inside a box headed "Turn to Summer" would have shown spring's gain, invited a change,
			      and then been wiped by the roll.

			      The step carries the group: the step describes the choice, and the group holds what
			      has been ticked. --}}
			{{#with pick.group}}
				<div class="steading-turn-pick">
					{{> "stonetop.choice-group" cgContext="steading"}}
				</div>
			{{/with}}
		{{/if}}

	</div>
</li>
